:answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15e9135b-b871-5aba-b40d-ba3025b92b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432557} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dbf6a1b-898e-56e9-9f12-6a5e1d80b975" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432575} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab65ccb-a918-513f-ad62-ae4b2896ed85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432558} {:answer/id #uuid "1e50b1fc-9bb0-519a-87b4-b41bfcf588bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ab65ccb-a918-513f-ad62-ae4b2896ed85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432558} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6385f95-6b30-5136-8980-da81fcd668ff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10c6f8f6-9fca-5e23-8276-22a855906488" :question/math-academy-id "q-334111" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=2{sin}^{2}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=1-{cos}^{2}⁡x,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}2{sin}^{2}⁡x & 1-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {(2{sin}^{2}⁡x)}^{′} & {(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2{sin}^{2}⁡x & 1-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(2{sin}^{2}⁡x)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =4({sin}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-4({sin}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd428707-bb32-58b2-8a51-09e330af0bb1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Independent Functions Using the Wronskian"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16a800c5-7e18-59ee-94e7-80cdfd4de19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305356} {:answer/id #uuid "a39ab598-0b8d-5c62-ad42-23748d01375c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16a800c5-7e18-59ee-94e7-80cdfd4de19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305356} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b09b08-7233-5ed9-8300-197e20e68046" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307724} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a221672-bcdc-5f01-bcdf-22405659444f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306315} {:answer/id #uuid "896dce62-f5b3-52c1-8dd8-bc6d2e4c1097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306465} {:answer/id #uuid "a265e633-dc0a-5983-8255-9b4e361560b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306677} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a73cc5-5dc5-51a3-9906-8c4d81c74c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1a73cc5-5dc5-51a3-9906-8c4d81c74c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307095} :answer-field/id #uuid "9248dce7-b98e-5ff4-bda2-e01c8b11790b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307983} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10fea060-278f-50a3-9b56-183bdf163c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305306} {:answer/id #uuid "d556e851-090a-5509-bf2f-41c8241ee8df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d556e851-090a-5509-bf2f-41c8241ee8df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307130} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2c59f88-c92c-51fe-84a6-89edef0e2468" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b67df1a9-5674-599d-941a-796b1c85ba70" :question/math-academy-id "q-334118" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=x+1,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}x+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{′} & ({x}^{2})^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}x+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(x+1)\\cdot (2x)-{x}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2x(x+1)-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2x-{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x \\\\  & =x(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12840b9d-f4f7-5e1e-9319-49c92b0a1514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432556} {:answer/id #uuid "75105097-da69-5af8-857c-8da170024488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432564} {:answer/id #uuid "7a565dca-df39-5e2d-b63f-4adbd2a8ea39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432565} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5318c9-c7a7-51b8-9b4d-6688604aeba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432566} {:answer/id #uuid "e87d37de-a054-5273-bb9c-f325114b3de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a565dca-df39-5e2d-b63f-4adbd2a8ea39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432565} :answer-field/id #uuid "750328c3-c578-5e21-8518-8f7c29a90ca6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8005871f-5013-58a1-b9ee-7ef831d491ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-334257" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}-12y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nII. ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nIII. The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{12}{{x}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(\\frac{12}{{x}^{5}})+2x(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})-12(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(6x)+2x(3{x}^{2})-12({x}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09ca4b98-0fe7-5c0e-bc3e-5d2f9d7b445d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432555} {:answer/id #uuid "61b68282-4cc9-5b93-be49-86af47b4a3b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432563} {:answer/id #uuid "b3726556-58cf-54f7-a94e-a01800ba1dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432567} {:answer/id #uuid "da777daa-3338-5846-a305-378dd1e86eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432568} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a6e302-ea43-50f3-855f-a828c356ceab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3726556-58cf-54f7-a94e-a01800ba1dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432567} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c538444-4b53-5d79-a4f2-bda9a48b5d41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "248767b0-4e8f-59a2-a18a-6d7a3f7d1129" :question/math-academy-id "q-334262" :question/problem "$$\n4{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+6x{y}^{′}-2y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$\n\nII. ${y}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$\n\nIII. The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x).$ Notice that $\\frac{1}{x}={x}^{-1},$ so we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})+6x(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})-2(\\frac{1}{x}) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{8}{x}-\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{2}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x).$ Notice that $\\sqrt{x}={x}^{\\frac{1}{2}},$ so we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2})+6x(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2})-2\\sqrt{x} & =0 \\\\ (-{x}^{1/2}+3{x}^{1/2}-2{x}^{1/2}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-1} & {x}^{1/2} \\\\ -{x}^{-2} & \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={x}^{-1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}-{x}^{1/2}\\cdot (-{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2}+{x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335741 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca68460-f3fe-5ae8-b5f4-85742c0ef46b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8de8f1-0aa5-5597-8f2a-04314cbb183e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333095} {:answer/id #uuid "15516e59-127e-5585-b223-c41ec895f21c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333176} {:answer/id #uuid "852d9dce-fad7-5d6d-9d52-31ed637d34a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334173} {:answer/id #uuid "8936b9da-7dc8-5edc-ba5b-5f96a52fcf44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334208} {:answer/id #uuid "e05757f5-0bd3-5d05-a456-6e255ec4d863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e05757f5-0bd3-5d05-a456-6e255ec4d863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334942} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b23fc9c-30a6-50f5-8daf-56d713fe0638" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e41bd3a2-77cd-50f9-af0d-7902e2113a96" :question/math-academy-id "q-64747" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =({e}^{x}){(x{e}^{x})}^{′}-(x{e}^{x}){({e}^{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x})-(x{e}^{x})({e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}-x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
